    lb = lambda r: P(lk[r]['base']['p'], 0)
    p(f"The probability of a change fell steeply with distance from the nearest threshold (Figure 4). For a first-visit risk exactly at 3%, 5% or 10%, the modelled chance of a different base category (π_clin) was {', '.join((P(x) + '%' for x in pi0[:2]))} and {P(pi0[2])}%; it was at least 25% for first-visit risks of {zz(ZB25['3.0'])}, {zz(ZB25['5.0'])} and {zz(ZB25['10.0'])} (instability zones) and at least 10% for {zz(ZB10['3.0'])}, {zz(ZB10['5.0'])} and {zz(ZB10['10.0'])}, but {lb(7)}% at 7%, at most {P(lk[2]['base']['p'])}% at risks of 2% or lower and {P(lk[15]['base']['p'])}% at 15% or higher (Table 3, a post hoc display of the prespecified model). Standardized to the eligible NHANES III population, and assuming that change depends only on distance from a threshold, {P(K4['theta_std'])}% (95% CI {P(K4['theta_std_ci'][0])}–{P(K4['theta_std_ci'][1])}%) would change base category (Supplementary Table S7); allowing the change SD to vary by age and race and ethnicity gave {P(X2['E7']['theta_std_het'])}% (post hoc; Supplementary Table S32).")
