def results_detail():
    return [blk for blk in results_full() if blk[0] != 'h1']

def discussion():
    b = []
    p = lambda s: b.append(('p', s))
    b.append(('h1', 'Discussion'))
    lo0, hi0 = (100 * min(pi0), 100 * max(pi0))
    p(f"When every routinely measured PREVENT input was remeasured a median of {iv['median']:.0f} days later under the same standardized protocol, {pr['events']} of {pr['n']} participants ({P(pr['estimate'])}%) changed base PREVENT-ASCVD category (Central Illustration). Changes went in both directions and came mainly from blood pressure, then lipids; laboratory remeasurement added instability beyond blood pressure, and adding a remeasured UACR did not clearly change stability. Changes concentrated near thresholds: for a first-visit risk at 3%, 5% or 10%, the modelled chance of a different category at the second visit was {lo0:.0f}–{hi0:.0f}%, roughly even. In Study 2, changing only the urine specimen changed the category for {P(pa['estimate'])}% of participants, usually downward and mostly without a change in albuminuria status; in a post hoc benchmark among the {bp12['n']:,} with two eligible blood pressure readings, the specimen change altered {P(ur12['estimate'])}% and swapping two readings taken minutes apart {P(bp12['estimate'])}%.")
    p(f"Proximity to a threshold explains most of the pattern. Although continuous agreement was excellent, nearly every first-visit risk from {F(ZB10['3.0'][0])}% to {F(ZB10['5.0'][1])}% carried at least a 10% chance of a change. Category stability therefore depends on the risk distribution as much as on the measurements, hence the threshold-distance curves and standardized estimate, both resting on assumptions. On a continuous scale, a second estimate would have to be more than about {P(rcv[1], 0)}% higher or {P(-rcv[0], 0)}% lower than the first before the difference exceeded test-retest variation.")
    p(f"Post hoc, the 2025 guideline's recommendation to start antihypertensive medication changed for {P(x3['theta_rx']['estimate'])}% of untreated participants, and {P(x3['p_not_rx2_given_rx1']['estimate'], 0)}% of those recommended medication at the first visit would not have been at the second. These changes came mainly through blood pressure stage ({x3['flips_with_stage_change']} of {x3['theta_rx']['events']}); the 7.5% criterion, which applies only within stage 1, rarely decided the recommendation alone. This supports the guideline's requirement for readings on two or more occasions. Multivariable risk has been argued to be less sensitive than single inputs to measurement error;[[rahimi2025]] PREVENT categories nonetheless changed for 1 in 10 participants, and, model-based, {P(x4['primary']['kappa'], 0)}% of single-visit categories differed from the usual level.")
    sv, s8 = (S7['sbp_variance'], S8['base'])
    bl, bh = (S12['B_base_low_cva'], S12['B_base_high_cva'])
    p(f"On the category scale, blood pressure was the largest single source, and between-day variation mattered more than variation between readings: a single reading on each day changed the category {s8['c_v1r2_vs_v2r2']['estimate'] / s8['a_v1_r1_vs_r2']['estimate']:.1f}–{s8['c_v1r2_vs_v2r2']['estimate'] / s8['b_v1_r2_vs_r3']['estimate']:.1f} times as often as swapping readings within a visit, so averaging readings within one visit cannot remove the day-to-day component. Lipids contributed as much as blood pressure on the continuous scale. For the laboratory inputs, analytical error alone (model-based) produced only {bl['ratio_to_Dsym_lab']:.2f}–{bh['ratio_to_Dsym_lab']:.2f} times the observed laboratory-only proportion. Because category changes are not additive, this ratio is not the share of changes caused by analytical error, but it suggests that most laboratory-driven instability arose from within-person biological or pre-analytical variation, which more precise assays alone would not remove; the high-CV upper limit ({bh['ratio_ci'][1]:.3f}) only just met the prespecified rule, and these intervals understate uncertainty because the simulation SDs were held fixed.")
    p(f"Designs that remeasure one input understate category instability. In the same participants, remeasuring cholesterol and diabetes as well as SBP raised the proportion crossing the PCE 7.5% threshold from {P(S11['cross75_sbp_only']['estimate'])}% to {P(S11['cross75_full']['estimate'])}%, extending the SBP-only analysis of Ye and colleagues.[[ye2014]] Within-visit reading swaps, as in ARIC[[ogunwale2016]] and a recent abstract using PREVENT-CVD,[[benealhasan2026]] changed {P(s8['b_v1_r2_vs_r3']['estimate'])}–{P(s8['a_v1_r1_vs_r2']['estimate'])}% of categories in Study 1, against {P(s8['c_v1r2_vs_v2r2']['estimate'])}% for a single reading on another day ({F(100 * s8['c_minus_b']['estimate'])} pp more than the reading 2 vs 3 swap; 95% CI {rng(s8['c_minus_b']['lower'], s8['c_minus_b']['upper'])}) and {P(pr['estimate'])}% when all inputs were remeasured (Figure 6C); these comparisons involve different equations, eras and populations (Table 4).")
    wv = [100 * s8['a_v1_r1_vs_r2']['estimate'], 100 * s8['b_v1_r2_vs_r3']['estimate'], 100 * bp12['estimate']]
    p(f"The urine specimen is a source of category change of the same order as the other inputs. Changing the specimen ({P(pa['estimate'])}%), or remeasuring random urine on another day ({P(K2U['single_swap']['URINE']['ref1'])}% in Study 1; {P(ex['estimate'])}%, 95% CI {cin(ex)}, in the imprecise 2001–2002 sample), changed the category in proportions of a similar order to swapping blood pressure readings within a visit or using a single reading on another day, and lower than remeasuring all inputs; these designs were not compared formally. Our analysis translates the collection-strategy differences in albuminuria shown by Saydah and colleagues[[saydah2013]] into PREVENT risk categories; after removing the average shift, {P(X2['E8']['shift_removed_any']['estimate'])}% still changed category, now in both directions (post hoc). Most category changes ({P(fbs['estimate'], 0)}%) occurred in participants with both UACRs below 30 mg/g, largely because they made up {P(fb['estimate'], 0)}% of the cohort, so an unchanged albuminuria flag does not ensure an unchanged cardiovascular risk category. Kidney Disease: Improving Global Outcomes (KDIGO) guidance recommends confirming an elevated random UACR with a first-morning specimen for kidney assessment;[[kdigo2024]] kidney assessment and cardiovascular risk classification should therefore be interpreted separately.")
    p('The results suggest that a single risk estimate close to a category boundary is better treated as a range, and Table 3 gives a first estimate of how likely a repeat measurement is to change it; in post hoc model-based simulations, a second day of measurements only near thresholds achieved most of the reduction in disagreement obtained by repeating everyone (Supplementary Figure S2). These findings concern measurement, not prediction: neither measurement is a reference, neither study has outcomes, and PREVENT was derived from single measurements, so some regression dilution is already built into its calibration. A category change is also not necessarily a change in treatment: statin recommendations also depend on LDL cholesterol, risk enhancers, coronary calcium and shared decision-making, and we analysed only the 10-year base, UACR and total CVD equations. Whether targeted repetition improves decisions or outcomes needs separate study.')
    e1b, e1s = (E1['base']['theta_lab']['estimate'], E1['study1_comparator']['base_lab_only_ref1']['estimate'])
    p(f"Study 1 remeasured all routinely measured inputs under one frozen protocol, with independent recomputation and complete reporting of prespecified estimands, but has three main limitations. First, the second examination was a nonrandom convenience subsample without weights, so the crude proportion is not a population prevalence; participants without complete pairs were less often non-Hispanic White and had higher blood pressure and risk, and the prespecified completeness weighting did not include blood pressure or risk; and standardization assumes that change depends only on distance from a threshold, whereas the SD of the change in the linear predictor differed by age and by race and ethnicity, although allowing for this changed the standardized estimate by only {S(X2['E7']['difference_pp'])} pp (95% CI {F(X2['E7']['difference_ci_pp'][0])} to {F(X2['E7']['difference_ci_pp'][1])}; post hoc, nine age-by-race cells). Second, the measurements are from 1988–1994: physician mercury blood pressure, recorded to even numbers by the same teams over a short interval, probably understates the variation of routine office measurement, whereas older assays, especially kinetic Jaffe creatinine, may overstate contemporary analytical noise, and the result was sensitive to the creatinine calibration.[[selvin2007]] Third, with fewer than 100 changes, the category-scale contributions and paired differences are imprecise, and clustering on the examining physician widened the primary interval to {cin(X2['E5']['visit1_physician'])} (wild cluster bootstrap {P(X2['E5']['visit1_physician_wild_bootstrap']['lower'])}–{P(X2['E5']['visit1_physician_wild_bootstrap']['upper'])}%; post hoc). In addition, the decision analysis used office readings without out-of-office confirmation, a single urine specimen for CKD and no clinical cardiovascular disease criterion; smoking, medication use and self-reported diabetes came from a single interview, so their instability is not captured; only self-reported cardiovascular disease was excluded; few participants took statins; a single HbA1c criterion is not a clinical diagnosis; clamping out-of-domain inputs slightly attenuates variation; participants referred for prompt attention were not purely retested, although excluding them changed little; uncertainty in the calibration, coefficients and analytical CVs was not propagated; and masked sampling units only approximate the clustering of location, staff and laboratory runs.")
    p(f"Study 2 used measured paired specimens with the full survey design but has its own limitations. Collection time, setting and handling changed together, and individual collection intervals were not released, so the design cannot separate their effects or distinguish biological from analytical variation. Its eligibility was amended after a pilot, and it was not preregistered. Self-reported history and treatment, incomplete baseline predictors, missing second specimens ({rw['nonresponders_n']} of {rw['target_n']:,}; extreme bounds {P(rw['extreme_missing_outcome_bounds'][0])}–{P(rw['extreme_missing_outcome_bounds'][1])}%) and pregnancy disclosure limited to the public release ages limit applicability, and response weighting addresses only selection captured by the measured covariates. NHANES 2009–2010 is the only public cycle with both specimens;[[nh_albcr_f]] the 2011–2012 file is available only through the NCHS Research Data Center.[[nh_alcr_g_r]] PREVENT was developed with spot UACR without a specified collection time,[[khan2024]] so neither specimen uniquely matches the derivation input, and without outcomes neither can be called the better risk input.")
    b.append(('h1', 'Conclusions'))
    p('About 1 in 10 participants changed PREVENT-ASCVD risk category when every routinely measured input was remeasured about 2 weeks later under a standardized protocol. Changes clustered near category thresholds and arose mainly from blood pressure and lipids; for the laboratory inputs, model-based simulations suggested that analytical imprecision alone would produce only a minority of the observed changes. Changing only the urine specimen changed the category of about 1 in 20, often without a change in albuminuria status. Risk estimates close to a threshold should be interpreted with this measurement uncertainty in mind.')
    return b

